 ### Picture shape
 
-Games whose profile supports it (Breath of the Wild, Animal Crossing: New Horizons). **iPhone Duo screen (1.43:1)** renders a taller picture of the Duo's own shape: the sides of 16:9 with more above and below, and the HUD at the screen's edges. **16:9** is the Switch's picture. The taller picture has about a quarter more pixels, so it runs a little slower and warmer.
+Games whose profile supports it (Breath of the Wild, Animal Crossing: New Horizons); the Mac settings window calls it **Aspect ratio**, with **16:9**, **16:10** and **Auto (match the screen)**, the default: the main screen's shape below a MacBook's notch when it is taller than 16:9, so 16:10 on a MacBook and 16:9 on most monitors. **iPhone Duo screen (1.43:1)** (iOS) renders a taller picture of the Duo's own shape: the sides of 16:9 with more above and below, and the HUD at the screen's edges. **16:9** is the Switch's picture. The taller picture has about a quarter more pixels, so it runs a little slower and warmer.
 
 ### Wide screen
 
-Mac, games whose build supports it (Ocarina of Time). **Match screen**, the default, renders a picture of the main screen's own shape when it is wider than 16:9 (an ultrawide monitor, 3440x1440 for one): the same view up and down, more of the scene at the sides, and the HUD at the screen's edges. On a 16:9 or taller screen it is the Switch's 16:9 picture, as **16:9**. The game's window opens in that shape; choose **Fullscreen** in [Display mode](#display-mode) to fill the screen. The wider picture has about a third more pixels, so it runs a little slower. **Handheld** [console mode](#console-mode) keeps 16:9. In an `env.txt`: `DM_SCREEN_WIDE=auto`, `0`, or a size such as `3440x1440`.
+Mac, games whose build supports it (Ocarina of Time); the Mac settings window calls it **Aspect ratio**. **Auto (match the screen)**, the default, renders a picture of the main screen's own shape (below a MacBook's notch, so 16:10 there) when it is wider than 16:9 (an ultrawide monitor, 3440x1440 for one): the same view up and down, more of the scene at the sides, and the HUD at the screen's edges; or when it is taller, from 1.3:1 (a MacBook's 16:10): the same height with a little less at the sides, filling the screen without bars. On a 16:9 screen it is the Switch's 16:9 picture, as **16:9**. **16:10** always renders 16:10 (2304x1440 docked). The game's window opens in that shape; choose **Fullscreen** in [Display mode](#display-mode) to fill the screen. The wider picture has about a third more pixels, so it runs a little slower. **Handheld** [console mode](#console-mode) keeps 16:9. In an `env.txt`: `DM_SCREEN_WIDE=auto`, `0`, or a size such as `3440x1440` or `16x10`.
 
